> 
> This is much easier to scan than repeating “2050” and “2100” in every column label.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Better still: use a figure for the main result
> 
> Because the estimates span orders of magnitude—from roughly $10^{-4}$ to several percentage points, with wide uncertainty intervals—a **dot-and-interval plot** may communicate the findings better than a giant table:
> 
> - rows: outcomes;
> - columns/facets: 2025, 2035, 2050 (1.7–2.1°C), and 2100 (2.4–3.0°C);
> - point: central estimate;
> - horizontal line: uncertainty interval.
> 
> Then the main text can include:
> 
> 1. a figure showing patterns across outcomes and time;
> 2. a compact table with selected numerical values or rankings;
> 3. the complete table in an appendix.
> 
> I would avoid a log x-axis unless you carefully handle negative intervals and zeros. A signed/asinh transformation is possible, but is probably more confusing for a policy-facing report.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Formatting details I would adopt
> 
> - State in the caption exactly what the values mean and what brackets denote, e.g. “Values are estimated percentage-point impacts; brackets indicate the 5th–95th percentile interval.” Adjust to your actual definition.
> - Distinguish **zero** from **not estimated**:
>   - `0` = modeled estimate is exactly zero;
>   - `—` = unavailable / not modeled.
> - Use commas in intervals rather than repeated hyphens:  
>   `0.0048 [0, 0.011]`, not `0.0048 [0 - 0.011]`.
> - Standardize precision. For example, either use 2–3 significant figures throughout or a defined threshold:
>   - values $\geq 0.01$: 2 significant digits;
>   - values $< 0.01$: 2–3 significant digits;
>   - retain scientific notation for genuinely tiny values if needed.
> - Consider shortening a few row labels:
>   - “Electricity demand & supply”
>   - “Electricity infrastructure”
>   - “Transport infrastructure”
>   - “Coastal infrastructure”
> 
