> 
> **Panel C. Near-term estimates (all warming levels)**
> 
> | Outcome | 2025 | 2035 |
> |---|---:|---:|
> | Agriculture | 0.0013 [−0.001, 0.004] | 0.0026 [−0.001, …] |
> | … | … | … |
> 
> That gives the report a clean narrative:
> 
> 1. **Damages are already present and generally increase into the near future** (2025–2035);
> 2. **Future damages differ materially across warming levels** (2050 and especially 2100);
> 3. Therefore, **holding warming to lower levels can reduce future damages**.
> 
> ## Use a figure as the headline evidence
> 
> For this substantive message, I would make the table supporting material and lead with a plot. In particular, make a **2100 low- versus high-warming comparison figure**.
> 
> A useful design would be a horizontal dot-and-whisker plot:
> 
> - **Rows:** outcomes;
> - **x-axis:** estimated damage;
> - **colors:** warming bins, progressing from cool green/blue (low warming) through orange/red (high warming);
> - **points and whiskers:** central estimates and uncertainty intervals;
> - **facets:** 2050 and 2100.
> 
> As coverage improves, each outcome will acquire a sequence of points running across temperature bins. The visual message is powerful where damages rise with warming: lower-warming points lie to the left of higher-warming points.
> 
> Alternatively, plot an explicit **difference relative to a high-warming reference**, such as
> 
> \[
> \Delta D_{i} = D_{i,\text{high warming}} - D_{i,\text{low warming}},
> \]
> 
> where positive values represent damages avoided under the lower-warming condition. This is even more directly aligned with your message, but I would only use it when the scenario pairings are sufficiently comparable and you can propagate uncertainty appropriately.
> 
> ## Be careful with the wording of the claim
> 
> If these are scenario-conditioned estimates rather than a fully causal policy experiment, phrase the conclusion as something like:
> 
> > Projected damages are generally lower in lower-warming scenarios, illustrating the potential for mitigation to reduce future climate damages.
> 
> Or, more strongly if your model design supports it:
> 
> > Limiting warming from the higher to the lower temperature range reduces projected damages for most modeled outcomes by 2100.
> 
> I would avoid implying monotonicity across *every* sector or cell—especially given uncertainty, adaptation, nonlinear effects, and tipping-point results. The appropriate overarching claim may be **lower expected aggregate damages**, rather than “every individual impact declines at every lower warming level.”
> 
> ## Design for completion now
> 
> Since you expect to fill the matrix, I would retain all scenario columns in the underlying data and design the table/figure as though it were complete:
> 
> - preserve the ordered warming bins;
> - use `—` for “not yet estimated,” never `<NA>`;
> - explicitly distinguish `0` from `—`;
> - use muted gray text or light gray fill for unavailable cells;
> - do not condition visual inclusion on present-day completeness.
