> 
> ## Export both tables to one Word document
> 
> The most reliable route for preserving `gt` tables in Word is **Quarto** (or R Markdown), rather than trying to append individual exported `.docx` files.
> 
> Create a file called, for example, `damage_tables.qmd`:
> 
> ````markdown
> ---
> title: "Climate damage projections"
> format:
>   docx:
>     toc: false
> ---
> 
> ```{r}
> #| echo: false
> 
> library(dplyr)
> library(tidyr)
> library(gt)
> 
> # Source or run the code that creates:
> # near.term
> # long.term
> # near_term_gt
> # long_term_gt
> ```
> 
> ## Near-term projections
> 
> ```{r}
> #| echo: false
> near_term_gt
> ```
> 
> \newpage
> 
> ## Long-term projections
> 
> ```{r}
> #| echo: false
> long_term_gt
> ```
> ````
> 
> Then render it from R:
> 
> ```r
> quarto::quarto_render("damage_tables.qmd")
> ```
> 
> This produces:
> 
> ```text
> damage_tables.docx
> ```
> 
> Alternatively, from a terminal:
> 
> ```bash
> quarto render damage_tables.qmd
> ```
> 
> For a report that may become a standalone Word document, this is preferable because you can add explanatory text, methods notes, figure captions, page breaks, and references around the tables in the same reproducible source file.
> 
> One small substantive suggestion: change the note’s “central estimate” language if these are medians specifically—e.g., “Entries report median estimated damages; brackets give the 5th–95th percentile interval.”
